# Keep it out of search engines and prevent public caching
<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow, noarchive"
  Header set Cache-Control "no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate, private"
</IfModule>

# ============================================================================
# PASSWORD PROTECTION — currently OFF. Turn it on before trusting this folder.
# ============================================================================
#
# WHAT IS EXPOSED RIGHT NOW
#   Anyone who knows this URL can read journal.json — your real fills, entry
#   and exit prices, and P&L. The headers above stop Google indexing the page.
#   They do not stop a person. Verified 2026-08-03: journal.json was fetched
#   with no credentials at all.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS MORE NOW
#   notes.php in this folder WRITES to disk. Apache checks this file before any
#   script runs, so Basic Auth protects reading and writing together. With it
#   off, anyone who finds the URL can write into your journal.
#
#   Putting a token in the page's JavaScript instead would not help — it would
#   be sitting in view-source for the same person to read.
#
# SETUP — one time, and you have to do it: I cannot create a password file, and
# you should never paste a password into a chat.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Easiest: hPanel -> Advanced -> Password Protect Directories, pointed at
#        public_html/mishra-ventures/discipline-journal/my
#    That writes .htpasswd for you — then skip to step 4.
#
#    To do it by hand instead, continue:
#
# 2. Create the password file OUTSIDE public_html so it can never be served:
#        /home/<your-user>/.htpasswd-journal
#    hPanel -> Files -> File Manager, or over FTP. Hostinger's password-protect
#    tool can generate the hashed line for you to paste in.
#
# 3. Uncomment the four lines below and set AuthUserFile to that FULL path.
#    A relative path will not work.
#
# 4. Test in a private window. You should get a browser password prompt for
#    BOTH of these, before any content appears:
#        /mishra-ventures/discipline-journal/my/
#        /mishra-ventures/discipline-journal/my/journal.json
#    If journal.json opens with no prompt, it is still public. Fix that before
#    writing anything you would mind a stranger reading.
#
# Once it is on, notes sync with no extra login: the browser sends the same
# credentials to notes.php that it already used for the page.

#AuthType Basic
#AuthName "My Journal"
#AuthUserFile /home/REPLACE-WITH-YOUR-USER/.htpasswd-journal
#Require valid-user

# Never serve the notes store as a plain file, whatever else is configured.
# notes.php is the only way in or out, and it validates what it writes.
<Files "notes.json">
  Require all denied
</Files>
